src.contrast.json:
{
  "$description": "CONTRAST CONTRACT — which semantic roles get painted on which, and what each pairing owes. Checked by @substrate/validate across all four brand×theme modes on every run of `pnpm validate`, CI, and pre-commit. This file exists because every contrast fix in this system's history was found the same way: ship it, run axe against the rendered page, discover white-on-signal-500 is 4.16:1, patch the token. That loop works but it only finds what happens to be on screen, and it finds it last. A pairing declared here is checked before anything renders.",
  "levels": {
    "text": {
      "$description": "WCAG 2.1 AA for body text. Applies to any pairing where one role is ink and the other is the ground it is read on.",
      "min": 4.5
    },
    "ui": {
      "$description": "WCAG 2.1 AA for non-text: focus indicators, control boundaries, and data marks — anything that must be locatable without being read.",
      "min": 3
    },
    "hairline": {
      "$description": "A visible edge — explicitly NOT an accessibility affordance, and named so nobody reads it as one. `input` is the resting outline shared by every outlined control (input, textarea, select, checkbox, radio, outline button, toggle, choice-card): one token, eleven components. Holding it to WCAG 1.4.11's 3:1 forces neutral.500, three steps darker than `border`, and every control becomes a hard grey box against hairline cards — technically compliant, visibly incoherent, the wrong trade. An earlier revision here set 2.0 instead, which was worse than either option: a number invented to make a compromise feel principled. The honest position is that a resting 1px outline is decoration, and identification comes from the things that actually carry it — the label (always paired, enforced), the placeholder, the control's shape, and on interaction a 3px `ring` that clears 3:1 with room. This floor only guarantees the edge is *visible*. If a user reports the resting state is hard to find, that is evidence and it should move the token.",
      "min": 1.3
    },
    "distinct": {
      "$description": "Not a WCAG level, and deliberately not a perceptual threshold. It asserts only that two roles which are supposed to differ are not the SAME value. A contrast ratio cannot tell you whether a white card reads as a card on near-white paper — border and shadow carry that, and the token graph cannot see them. What it can tell you is that background == card == #FFFFFF, which is what the `ia` skin shipped in rev 1: three depth cues deleted at once, every correctness gate green. Setting a higher floor here would fail the legitimate paper/card relationship and teach people to add exceptions, which is how a gate stops meaning anything.",
      "min": 1.01
    }
  },
  "pairs": [
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "Reading ink on every ground it is set on.",
      "fg": [
        "foreground"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "background",
        "canvas",
        "surface",
        "surface-raised",
        "surface-overlay",
        "surface-sunken",
        "card",
        "popover",
        "secondary",
        "muted",
        "accent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "Scoped ink roles against their own ground.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "card-foreground",
          "card"
        ],
        [
          "popover-foreground",
          "popover"
        ],
        [
          "sidebar-foreground",
          "sidebar"
        ],
        [
          "prompt-bar-foreground",
          "prompt-bar"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "The supporting ink ladder. text-tertiary recedes but is still read, so it owes the same 4.5:1 as body — 'de-emphasised' is a size and weight decision, not a licence to drop contrast. NOTE the deliberate absence of `surface-raised`/`surface-overlay` here: the tonal-elevation param (FR-20) lifts those grounds toward mid-tone as they rise, and in dark mode a rising ground and light-on-dark supporting ink are in direct tension — tertiary clears 4.5:1 on neutral.900/800 and cannot on 700/600. Following M3, the guarantee on the two highest tonal surfaces is BODY ink (`foreground`, checked in the first group on every ground) — supporting ink is a `surface`-and-below role by design. What keeps this honest rather than a loophole is that the tonal step is bounded so `foreground` itself still clears on the highest surface (see tonal-elevation.test.mjs), and the step direction/magnitude are picked against exactly that floor.",
      "fg": [
        "text-secondary",
        "text-tertiary",
        "muted-foreground"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "background",
        "surface",
        "card"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "Structural labels sit on the sunken and muted grounds as often as on paper. `text-secondary` and `text-tertiary` are here as well as on paper because the landing specimen's stat band and every inset panel in the library set them on `surface-sunken` — a pairing the contract did not declare until FR-10's `inset` role map made a 4.02:1 out of it and an axe sweep found it that the gate could not. A pairing the system renders and the contract has never named is not covered by the gate; it is invisible to it.",
      "fg": [
        "muted-foreground",
        "text-secondary",
        "text-tertiary"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "muted",
        "surface-sunken",
        "secondary"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "Solid fills and their labels. Each of these is a *-foreground painted directly on its own role.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "primary-foreground",
          "primary"
        ],
        [
          "secondary-foreground",
          "secondary"
        ],
        [
          "accent-foreground",
          "accent"
        ],
        [
          "interactive-foreground",
          "interactive"
        ],
        [
          "destructive-foreground",
          "destructive"
        ],
        [
          "success-foreground",
          "success"
        ],
        [
          "warning-foreground",
          "warning"
        ],
        [
          "info-foreground",
          "info"
        ],
        [
          "destructive-solid-foreground",
          "destructive-solid"
        ],
        [
          "success-solid-foreground",
          "success-solid"
        ],
        [
          "warning-solid-foreground",
          "warning-solid"
        ],
        [
          "info-solid-foreground",
          "info-solid"
        ],
        [
          "sidebar-primary-foreground",
          "sidebar-primary"
        ],
        [
          "sidebar-accent-foreground",
          "sidebar-accent"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "The brand axis carries its own contrast color across every skin.",
      "fg": [
        "brand-contrast"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "brand-base",
        "brand-strong",
        "brand-emphasis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "Status color used as INK on its own tinted ground — the alert/callout recipe the no-side-rule rule mandates. This is the pairing that broke twice (light warning, light destructive) and it broke because the ramps were not aligned.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "destructive",
          "destructive-muted"
        ],
        [
          "success",
          "success-muted"
        ],
        [
          "warning",
          "warning-muted"
        ],
        [
          "info",
          "info-muted"
        ],
        [
          "interactive",
          "interactive-muted"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "Status color used as ink on ordinary grounds — an inline error message, a success note on a card.",
      "fg": [
        "destructive",
        "success",
        "warning",
        "info"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "background",
        "card"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "ui",
      "why": "Focus indicator and selection fill — WCAG 1.4.11. The ring must be locatable against every ground it can appear over.",
      "fg": [
        "ring",
        "interactive"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "background",
        "surface",
        "card",
        "popover"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "hairline",
      "why": "The shared resting outline of every outlined control, held below 1.4.11 on purpose — see the `hairline` level for the reasoning and for what actually carries identification.",
      "fg": [
        "input"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "background",
        "surface",
        "card",
        "popover"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "ui",
      "why": "Data marks must be findable against the ground they are plotted on. A chart series that disappears is not a styling nit — it is missing information.",
      "fg": [
        "chart-1",
        "chart-2",
        "chart-3",
        "chart-4",
        "chart-5"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "background",
        "card"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "distinct",
      "why": "Figure/ground — craft gate #1. A container that resolves to the same value as the page behind it has no edge at all. `surface-raised` vs `surface` is deliberately NOT here: in light mode both are white and elevation is carried by shadow, which is a legitimate choice this check cannot see.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "card",
          "background"
        ],
        [
          "popover",
          "background"
        ],
        [
          "surface-sunken",
          "surface"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "distinct",
      "why": "A line must differ from what it divides. `border` is checked against the grounds it encloses rather than against `popover` — a popover's border faces the page, not its own interior — but `divider` is checked inside popovers too, because menu separators are drawn on that ground.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "border",
          "background"
        ],
        [
          "border",
          "surface"
        ],
        [
          "border",
          "card"
        ],
        [
          "divider",
          "background"
        ],
        [
          "divider",
          "surface"
        ],
        [
          "divider",
          "card"
        ],
        [
          "divider",
          "popover"
        ],
        [
          "prompt-bar-line",
          "prompt-bar"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "text",
      "why": "DECK (FR-32) — the deck's reading ink on every ground a deck paints it on. `deck-ink` is the Statement / ActionTitle / StatBlock-value ink; `deck-mute` is the SourceLine / PageFooter / Legend-label register (one step quiet, still read at distance, so it owes the same 4.5:1 as body — a footnote nobody can read is a missing citation); `deck-positive`/`deck-negative` are the StatBlock and ComparisonColumn delta marks, which read as text (a `+12%` is a number, not a swatch). All ride the shared semantic ramps (FR-26), so these hold across light / dark / ia-light / ia-dark, not just the single light mode the old deck was welded to. `deck-wash` is a ground for `deck-ink` only — `deck-mute` on `deck-wash` collides in dark (both land on the same neutral step), and the wash carries body ink, not the quiet register.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "deck-ink",
          "deck-paper"
        ],
        [
          "deck-ink",
          "deck-surface"
        ],
        [
          "deck-ink",
          "deck-card"
        ],
        [
          "deck-ink",
          "deck-wash"
        ],
        [
          "deck-mute",
          "deck-paper"
        ],
        [
          "deck-mute",
          "deck-surface"
        ],
        [
          "deck-mute",
          "deck-card"
        ],
        [
          "deck-positive",
          "deck-paper"
        ],
        [
          "deck-positive",
          "deck-surface"
        ],
        [
          "deck-positive",
          "deck-card"
        ],
        [
          "deck-negative",
          "deck-paper"
        ],
        [
          "deck-negative",
          "deck-surface"
        ],
        [
          "deck-negative",
          "deck-card"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "ui",
      "why": "DECK (FR-32) — deck data marks must be findable against the ground they are plotted on, the same 3:1 non-text floor the UI `chart-*` swatches owe. `deck-signal`/`deck-ember` are the presentation-register accents (Kicker, chart emphasis) and `deck-series-1`…`deck-series-5` are the ChartFrame / Legend swatches; they are the bright chart ramps, NOT body ink, so they are held to `ui` and not `text` — a 3.34:1 bright blue is a legible mark and an illegible paragraph, and the deck uses it as the former. A deck series that vanishes into the paper is missing information on a slide, which is the failure this pairing exists to catch before an agent ever composes with it.",
      "fg": [
        "deck-signal",
        "deck-ember",
        "deck-series-1",
        "deck-series-2",
        "deck-series-3",
        "deck-series-4",
        "deck-series-5"
      ],
      "bg": [
        "deck-paper",
        "deck-surface",
        "deck-card"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "distinct",
      "why": "DECK (FR-32) — a deck rule (ChartFrame border, Legend divider, StepNode connector) must differ from the ground it is drawn on, the same `distinct` floor the UI `border`/`divider` owe. Checked against paper / surface / card, the three grounds a rule is actually drawn on. NOT checked against `deck-wash` — rule and wash both alias mid-neutral steps that collide to 1:1 in dark mode, and a wash is a fill behind a block, not a surface a divider is stroked on. Figure/ground for `deck-card`/`deck-surface` against `deck-paper` is deliberately ABSENT for the same reason `surface-raised` vs `surface` is absent in the UI set: in light mode both resolve near-white and the edge is carried by the rule and the shadow, which this check cannot see and should not fail.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "deck-rule",
          "deck-paper"
        ],
        [
          "deck-rule",
          "deck-surface"
        ],
        [
          "deck-rule",
          "deck-card"
        ]
      ]
    }
  ]
}

src.deck.tokens.json:
{
  "$description": "Deck surface — presentation scale for 1920x1080. First-class token group. Deck type is DERIVED (FR-27), not authored, and since FR-96 it is anchored on DECK CONVENTIONS at the reference frame rather than on the web scale: titles ~1/9 of frame height, body 34–40, stat metrics in the 120–200 class. The scale derives from its own regime in `primitive.deckTypeScale`. Consumed by the Figma deck kit sync and the Google Slides theme generator (slides-theme.json, FR-35).",
  "deck": {
    "type": {
      "$type": "typography",
