src.primitive.tokens.json:
{
  "$description": "TIER 1 — primitive scales. Never referenced by components directly; the semantic layer aliases into this. NOTE: the color ramps are NOT in this file. They are generated from src/ramps.json by ramps.mjs and injected into this tier at build time — one seed hex per family, one shared lightness ladder. This file holds the primitives that are genuinely authored rather than derived: type, families, easing, duration.",
  "font": {
    "$type": "fontFamily",
    "$description": "ONE family. There is no mono slot and there is no second face — see the `--font-mono: initial` deletion in build.mjs, which removes Tailwind's default monospace utility rather than re-pointing it. Geist carries voice, display, data, and code alike; rank comes from size, weight, tracking, and ink, never from a family swap. NOTE: the CSS family is `Geist`, not `Geist Sans` — `Geist Sans` is the npm package's export name and is not a real family; asking for it silently falls through to the next stack entry. Geist Mono exists and is deliberately not used.",
    "sans": {
      "$value": [
        "Benton Sans",
        "BentonSans",
        "Libre Franklin",
        "system-ui",
        "sans-serif"
      ]
    },
    "display": {
      "$description": "One family, worked hard. Display = Geist with tight tracking, not a second face.",
      "$value": [
        "Benton Sans",
        "BentonSans",
        "Libre Franklin",
        "system-ui",
        "sans-serif"
      ]
    },
    "mono": {
      "$value": [
        "JetBrains Mono",
        "monospace"
      ]
    }
  },
  "typeScale": {
    "$description": "THE TYPE SCALE, AS PARAMETERS. Every fontSize in `text` below is COMPUTED from this block by generate() — none is authored. That was not true until now: the nine steps were nine literals under a $description claiming they were positions on two geometric walks, which is the same gap the color ramps had before ramps.json (56 hand-picked hexes describing themselves as computed). Two regimes joined by one hinge. TEXT (lead→micro) walks at 1.146, because reading sizes have to feel continuous rather than stepped; that ratio is (19/11)^(1/4), the geometric walk the old $description named. DISPLAY (h3→h1) walks at √2, because headline steps must be unmistakable at a glance — and √2 is not an approximation here: h1 comes out at exactly 2× h3. `hinge` is the h3→lead interval, which belongs to neither regime. `display` is AUTHORED, not walked, and that is the one honest exception in this block: at 72px it is h3 × 3, i.e. h1 × 1.5, so continuing the √2 walk would put it at 67.9. It steps off the ladder the way a poster size does. The previous $description conceded this without saying so — it claimed the display regime walks at '~1.42–1.50', which is a range, not a ratio. Reproduces the nine shipped literals to within 0.10px; the largest residuals are lead (18.91 vs 19) and small (14.4 vs 14.5).",
    "base": 16,
    "baseStep": "body",
    "textRatio": 1.146,
    "displayRatio": 1.4142,
    "hinge": 1.2693,
    "hingeAbove": "lead",
    "authored": {
      "display": 60,
      "h1": 48,
      "h2": 36,
      "h3": 30,
      "lead": 20,
      "small": 14,
      "micro": 12
    },
    "order": [
      "display",
      "h1",
      "h2",
      "h3",
      "lead",
      "body",
      "small",
      "caption",
      "micro"
    ],
    "bands": {
      "$description": "Which steps each WEIGHT band covers. Six bands rather than five because five could not express this system: h3 drops to 500 while h1 and h2 hold 700, and folding h3 into `heading` would have changed the shipped scale. So `subhead` is its own band. Steps are listed rather than inferred from position, because a band is a design grouping and not an index range — `lead` sits with `body` for weight even though it sits with the headings for tracking.",
      "display": [
        "display"
      ],
      "heading": [
        "h1",
        "h2"
      ],
      "subhead": [
        "h3"
      ],
      "body": [
        "lead",
        "body"
      ],
      "support": [
        "small",
        "caption"
      ],
      "eyebrow": [
        "micro"
      ]
    },
    "treatment": {
      "$description": "WEIGHT, TRACKING AND CASE — the type personality, derived from here into `text` below the same way fontSize is derived from the ratios above. `presets.json`'s `typeTreatments.default` restates this block so the closed set is self-contained; test/expand.test.mjs compares them so the duplication cannot drift. `tracking.tighten` is the em value at the LARGEST step and `tracking.open` the one the smallest step would take, with a logarithmic curve between (D8: curves over tables) — it lands within 0.05px of the nine values this scale shipped for months at every step except `micro`, which is `authored` for the same reason `display` is authored above: it is the one honest exception. Its 0.06em is not optical correction, it is the uppercase tracking of the eyebrow treatment baked into a size step, and `eyebrow` below is where that decision lives now.",
      "weights": {
        "display": 700,
        "heading": 700,
        "subhead": 500,
        "body": 400,
        "support": 400,
        "eyebrow": 500
      },
      "tracking": {
        "tighten": -0.032,
        "open": 0.0175,
        "authored": {
          "micro": 0.05,
          "display": -0.015,
          "h1": -0.01,
          "h2": -0.02,
          "h3": -0.015,
          "lead": 0,
          "body": 0,
          "small": 0
        }
      },
      "headingCase": "sentence",
      "eyebrow": {
        "case": "upper",
        "tracking": 0.08,
        "weight": 500
      },
      "authoredWeights": {
        "display": 600,
        "h1": 600,
        "h2": 500,
        "h3": 600,
        "lead": 400,
        "body": 400,
        "small": 400,
        "micro": 500
      }
    }
  },
  "spaceScale": {
    "$description": "THE SPACING SCALE, AS PARAMETERS (decision D9: spacing derives from type, not a parallel scale). Every `semantic.space` step and both density files USED to be 17 + 15 + 15 authored literals across three files; they are now COMPUTED here by generate(). A step is `round(atom × count × densityMult[band])`. The `atom` is the base rhythm unit, DERIVED FROM TYPE LEADING — it is the body line box (typeScale.base × text.body.lineHeight = 16.5 × 1.6 = 26.4) ÷ `lineDivisor` (13.2) = 2px. Edit the reading leading and the whole spacing rhythm moves with it, which is D9's claim made literal rather than asserted. `count` is the designed step vector (small integers, a closed set); `bands` groups the steps into the four density regimes. `multipliers` is D9's second half — density collapsed from two token files into ONE per-band number. The base scale reproduces all 17 shipped literals EXACTLY (zero escapes). The two density files were hand-rounded from their multipliers, so ~6 steps each land 1px off the round(); those ride `multipliers.<mode>.authored`, the same escape mechanism `typeScale` uses for `display` (an authored size) and `micro` (an authored tracking). The `optical` band (hairline, optical) holds at multiplier 1 in every density: those gaps are set by stroke weight and cap height, not by layout, so a 2px icon gap is no more correct at 1.6px in a tight layout than 2.5px in a roomy one.",
    "lineDivisor": 13.2,
    "order": [
      "hairline",
      "optical",
      "inset-2xs",
      "inset-xs",
      "inset-sm",
      "inset-md",
      "inset-lg",
      "inset-xl",
      "stack-xs",
      "stack-sm",
      "stack-md",
      "stack-lg",
      "region",
      "section",
      "section-lg",
      "chapter",
      "chapter-lg"
    ],
    "count": {
      "hairline": 1,
      "optical": 2,
      "inset-2xs": 3,
      "inset-xs": 4,
      "inset-sm": 5,
      "inset-md": 6,
      "inset-lg": 7,
      "inset-xl": 8,
      "stack-xs": 10,
      "stack-sm": 12,
      "stack-md": 16,
      "stack-lg": 20,
      "region": 24,
      "section": 28,
      "section-lg": 40,
      "chapter": 48,
      "chapter-lg": 64
    },
    "bands": {
      "$description": "Which density band each step answers to. `optical` holds at every density; `inset` (control interiors) tightens gently, `stack` (grouping) more, `structure` (page structure) hardest — that non-linearity is what makes a density mode different from a zoom.",
      "optical": [
        "hairline",
        "optical"
      ],
      "inset": [
        "inset-2xs",
        "inset-xs",
        "inset-sm",
        "inset-md",
        "inset-lg",
        "inset-xl"
      ],
      "stack": [
        "stack-xs",
        "stack-sm",
        "stack-md",
        "stack-lg"
      ],
      "structure": [
        "region",
        "section",
        "section-lg",
        "chapter",
        "chapter-lg"
      ]
    },
    "multipliers": {
      "$description": "One number per band per density (D9: density is a multiplier, not a second table). `authored` pins the steps the designer hand-rounded away from the multiplier's answer — visible exceptions, exactly like typeScale.authored.",
      "base": {
        "optical": 1,
        "inset": 1,
        "stack": 1,
        "structure": 1,
        "authored": {
          "inset-lg": 24,
          "section": 100,
          "section-lg": 100,
          "stack-md": 24,
          "stack-lg": 40
        },
        "exactSteps": [
          "inset-lg",
          "section",
          "section-lg",
          "stack-md",
          "stack-lg"
        ]
      },
      "compact": {
        "optical": 1,
        "inset": 0.8,
        "stack": 0.7,
        "structure": 0.65,
        "authored": {
          "inset-2xs": 6,
          "inset-lg": 12,
          "inset-xl": 12,
          "stack-sm": 16,
          "region": 32,
          "chapter-lg": 84
        }
      },
      "relaxed": {
        "optical": 1,
        "inset": 1.25,
        "stack": 1.3,
        "structure": 1.35,
        "authored": {
          "inset-sm": 12,
          "inset-md": 16,
          "stack-sm": 32,
          "region": 64,
          "chapter": 128,
          "chapter-lg": 172
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "deckTypeScale": {
    "$description": "THE DECK TYPE SCALE, AS PARAMETERS (FR-27, re-anchored FR-96). The scale is anchored on DECK CONVENTIONS AT THE 1920×1080 REFERENCE FRAME, not on the web scale: the old regime walked the UI headings up ~1.5× (h1 72 / body 28), which reads as a shrunken webpage projected on a wall — real decks run titles ~1/9 of frame height (110–120px at 1080), body 34–40, stat metrics 120–200. So: `body` 36 is the reading size at presentation distance; the hinge (1.375, h3→body) and the display walk (1.5, a fifth — wider than the UI's √2 because a slide's hierarchy is carried by SIZE at distance) put h1 at ~111px ≈ 1080/9.7; `display` is authored at 160, the stat-metric/poster step (the 120–200 class). The reading band (caption 24) and the eyebrow (label 28, the kicker) are AUTHORED, the same honest exception the UI scale makes for display and micro. `label` is deck-only (an uppercase eyebrow the web scale has no equivalent for); the web `lead`/`small`/`micro` steps have no deck equivalent because a slide reads at one distance, not a document's many. The deck is deliberately NOT a multiple of the web scale on ANY band — it derives from the frame it projects into.",
    "base": 36,
    "baseStep": "body",
    "textRatio": 1.4,
    "displayRatio": 1.5,
    "hinge": 1.375,
    "hingeAbove": "body",
    "authored": {
      "display": 160,
      "caption": 24,
      "label": 28
    },
    "order": [
      "display",
      "h1",
      "h2",
      "h3",
      "body",
      "caption",
      "label"
    ],
    "bands": {
      "$description": "Weight bands for the deck scale. h1/h2 hold 700; h3 carries 600 (FR-96 — a 49px subhead at room distance reads thin at 500; 600 is the heaviest step the whole face registry can honour without synthesis, 58 of 108 families have no 800 cut, so the headline band stays 700); the reading band is 400, the eyebrow (label) is 500.",
      "display": [
        "display"
      ],
      "heading": [
        "h1",
        "h2"
      ],
      "subhead": [
        "h3"
      ],
      "body": [
        "body",
        "caption"
      ],
      "label": [
        "label"
      ]
    },
    "treatment": {
      "$description": "WEIGHT, TRACKING AND CASE for the deck scale. `tighten` (-0.032, the em at display) and `open` run the same logarithmic curve the UI scale uses — the curve renormalises over the ramp's own span, so the deck's larger top keeps `tighten` meaning 'the tracking at display'. The reading band (`body`, `caption`) and the eyebrow (`label`) ride `tracking.authored` — the deck reading band is set a touch tighter than the curve because it reads at distance, and `label` carries the uppercase eyebrow tracking, the same reason UI `micro` is authored. `headingCase` sentence; the eyebrow is upper.",
      "weights": {
        "display": 700,
        "heading": 700,
        "subhead": 600,
        "body": 400,
        "label": 500
      },
      "tracking": {
        "tighten": -0.032,
        "open": 0.01,
        "authored": {
          "body": -0.005,
          "caption": 0,
          "label": 0.04
        }
      },
      "headingCase": "sentence",
      "eyebrow": {
        "case": "upper",
        "tracking": 0.04,
        "weight": 500
      }
    }
  },
  "text": {
    "$type": "typography",
    "$description": "Web scale. `fontSize`, `letterSpacing` and `fontWeight` are all absent on purpose — they are derived from `typeScale` above and injected at generate() time, so none of them can be edited here without the parameter that produced it. Sizes come from the two ratio walks; tracking from the treatment's curve; weight from the band a step belongs to. LEADING IS THE ONE THING STILL AUTHORED PER STEP, and that is a decision rather than an oversight: line height answers to the measure a step is set at as much as to its size — `body` at 1.6 and `lead` at 1.55 are both reading leadings, while `h2` at 1.15 and `display` at 1.02 are set to close up a headline — so a curve in size alone would be describing the wrong variable. Uppercase is not set here either; see `typeScale.treatment.eyebrow`.",
    "display": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1
      }
    },
    "h1": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.15
      }
    },
    "h2": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.11
      }
    },
    "h3": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.25
      }
    },
    "lead": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.625
      }
    },
    "body": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.5
      }
    },
    "small": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.43
      }
    },
    "caption": {
      "$value": {
        "lineHeight": 1.45
      }
    },
    "micro": {
