    "measure": {
      "$type": "dimension",
      "$description": "Line length — the one typographic decision the scale could not make for itself. Sizes and tracking were specified to three decimals while the variable that most decides whether prose reads was left to whatever max-w-* a surface reached for. Stated in `ch`, so a measure travels with the type size it wraps instead of freezing a pixel width: the same token is narrower in px at caption than at lead, which is the correct behaviour. Larger type wants FEWER characters per line, so headlines take the small end of this scale, not the large one. Deliberately absent from the density overlay — measure answers to the eye's saccade, not to layout, exactly like the optical space band.",
      "xs": {
        "$value": "34ch"
      },
      "sm": {
        "$value": "48ch"
      },
      "md": {
        "$value": "62ch"
      },
      "lg": {
        "$value": "72ch"
      }
    },
    "layout": {
      "$type": "dimension",
      "$description": "Page-frame widths — the max-width a full-bleed region's content band spends, distinct from `measure` (a reading constraint on prose in `ch`). A hero, a masthead, a footer, an announcement bar all cap their inner band at one of these so the whole page frame aligns to one edge. Stated in `rem` because a page frame is structure, not text: it scales with the root but does NOT ride the density overlay (rolling compact/relaxed retightens gaps and controls, not the width of the page) and does NOT travel with type size the way a `ch` measure does. `narrow` is for single-stack content (a centered hero); `page` is the standard content frame; `wide` is for dense dashboards that earn the extra span.",
