    "stroke": {
      "$type": "dimension",
      "$description": "HOW HEAVY A HAIRLINE IS. One token, and until FR-12 it was not a token at all — every rule, divider, card edge and resting input outline in the system was 1px forever, because `border` compiles to a literal. `.border` and its four sides are re-declared in the utilities layer to read this, so the whole library follows it with no per-site change. It is semantic rather than primitive because it is skinnable the way a colour role is: a brand whose edges are 2px is a real brand. Border is one of the three things carrying figure/ground — with shadow and with the card/background value difference — and the only one the contrast gate can partly see. `contrast.json`'s `hairline` level asks a border for 1.3:1 against its ground and that floor is calibrated for 1px: heavier carries an edge a lower-contrast colour could not, and 0.5px needs more contrast than the level asks for.",
      "hairline": {
        "$value": "1px"
      }
    },
    "radius": {
      "$type": "dimension",
      "$description": "Vanilla is soft (10px base ladder). Skins may flatten it — ia goes sharp.",
      "$roles": "controls / containers / inline / chip / track are radius BY ROLE (FR-18, D17): each names the ladder rung a component family stands on, so a system can round buttons and cards differently. These literals are the `uniform` preset (the checked-in per-component assignment: controls→md, containers→lg, inline→sm, chip→pill, track→pill) — the playground re-resolves them live when radiusRoles is rolled. `chip` (D17) is the badge/tag/filter-chip family: `pill` resolves to a 9999px capsule — the geometry components used to pin with rounded-full — and crisp role maps put it on a rung instead, which is how a skin ships rect chips. `track` (D17) is the linear-geometry family — progress/meter tracks and fills, slider rails, bar marks — same vocabulary, and it additionally derives `--cap-track` (round/butt) so SVG strokework follows the same decision. The sixth family, circular (avatars, dots, thumbs), is intentionally absent: it is rounded-full by geometry and pinned in the components, never a token.",
